      </div>
    </figure>
        <p>This guide covers the eight Chinese manufacturers that show up most often on buyer shortlists for zip up hoodie production, and how to judge which one matches your order size, quality bar, and launch calendar. The list is organized by sourcing scenario, not by an invented ranking. A workshop that handles a 500-piece streetwear drop beautifully is the wrong partner for a 50,000-piece retail program, and the reverse is just as true.</p>
        <p class="ar-note">Last updated: October 2026.</p>
        <h2 id="why-china">Why China Remains the Global Hub for Zip Up Hoodie Manufacturing</h2>
        <p>China's edge in hoodie production is not labor cost. Wages in the coastal apparel belts have climbed for over a decade and no longer undercut Vietnam or Bangladesh. The real advantage is cluster density. Inside a two to three hour drive of the main knit zones in Guangdong, Zhejiang, and Jiangsu, you can reach fabric mills, zipper and trim suppliers, dye houses, embroidery shops, and print studios. A zip up hoodie pulls four supply chains together at once: knit fabric, a functioning closure, rib trim, and surface decoration. That is why proximity matters more here than it does for a basic tee. China remains one of the largest apparel exporters by value, and knit garments carry a heavy share of that volume, so an overseas buyer finds range at both ends of the supply base: integrated OEM plants with in-house lab testing and audit files, and flexible workshops willing to run 300 pieces to prove out a design.</p>
        <p>Three consequences for your sourcing decision:</p>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Fabric weight and zipper grade drive cost, not sewing labor.</strong> Two quotes for the "same" hoodie can differ substantially, often well over 30%, when one is costed on 280gsm fleece with an unbranded nylon #3 and the other on 400gsm brushed-back fleece with a branded #5 metal zipper.</li><li><strong>Lead time hinges on trim availability.</strong> A non-standard zipper tape color means a dye lot and a lab dip approval before cutting starts, which adds two to three weeks.</li><li><strong>Flexibility is a function of factory size.</strong> Large integrated plants are engineered for long runs. Mid-size and specialist factories are engineered for changeovers.</li></ul>
