        <h2 id="key-specs">Zip Up Hoodie Manufacturing: Key Specs Buyers Should Understand</h2>
        <p>Most sourcing disputes trace back to a spec conversation nobody had. These are the specs that decide whether the bulk garment still matches your approved sample six months later.</p>
        <p><strong>Fabric and weight.</strong> Hoodies are built from fleece, french terry, or loopback, and weight is expressed in GSM. Lightweight runs roughly 240–280gsm, midweight 300–350gsm, heavyweight 380–450gsm and above. Brushed-back fleece feels softer and traps more warmth; french terry has a smoother face with a looped back and breathes better. Weight drives both cost and perceived quality. It is also the first thing a customer registers when they pick the garment up, which is why premium positioning almost always goes heavy. Lock a shrinkage tolerance in writing too; 3% on length is a reasonable ask on a pre-shrunk fleece. For construction trade-offs in detail, see <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/#fabric">zip up hoodie fabric options</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Zippers and trims.</strong> The zipper is the most scrutinized component on the garment and the most common source of returns. A misaligned slider shows up in the first QC pull, and inexperienced factories lose money here before they lose it anywhere else. The spread runs from unbranded nylon #3 to branded #5 metal, and the cost gap between them can exceed a dollar per piece. Specify tape color, teeth size, slider finish, pull style, and whether the zipper is reverse coil. Placket width and how the tape is set into the front panel decide whether the closure lies flat after laundering or waves.</p>
        <p><strong>Fit, ribbing, and decoration.</strong> Fit names such as regular, relaxed, oversized, and drop shoulder mean nothing without a measurement chart and grading rules attached. Rib at cuffs and hem controls recovery, and 2x2 rib with 5% spandex holds shape far longer than a plain 1x1 cotton rib. Decoration changes both hand-feel and durability: screen print is the cheapest route above roughly 300 pieces per design, DTG and DTF handle short runs and photographic art, embroidery buys perceived value, and wash treatments deliver vintage character at the price of batch-to-batch variation.</p>
